 "parts": [
  "Why is a marathon twenty-six point two miles?",
  "It is such a strange number.",
  "The first Olympic marathon, in eighteen ninety-six, ran about forty kilometers.",
  "That is roughly twenty-five miles.",
  "London, nineteen oh-eight.",
  "The course began at Windsor Castle and finished at the stadium.",
  "It came to twenty-six miles, plus three hundred eighty-five yards on the track.",
  "The famous story says the start was set for a royal nursery window.",
  "The records do not back that up.",
  "Newspaper notices show the organizers asked permission to start at Windsor.",
  "The royal family had not asked.",
  "In the stadium, an Italian runner, Dorando Pietri, staggered in first.",
  "Officials helped him. He was disqualified.",
  "Johnny Hayes, of the United States, got the gold.",
  "So was that distance chosen for a reason?",
  "Olympedia calls it arbitrary.",
  "In nineteen twenty-one, the athletics federation made it official.",
  "Those last three hundred eighty-five yards are London's. Save some cheering for them."
 ],
